// Plant nutrients as N, P2O5 and K2O (2026-09-26, gardening depth rung 3).
// Read by src/systems/farming/soil.rs, which carries the model and the
// reasoning; this file carries the numbers and where each one came from.
//
// UNITS. Every nutrient amount in the simulation is GRAMS of N, P2O5 and K2O,
// the convention fertilizer labels and extension-service removal tables use,
// so any number can be checked against a bag or a table without converting.
// (For the element: P = 0.436 x P2O5 and K = 0.830 x K2O, from molar masses.)
//
// -- demand_anchor: what a crop takes out of its unit in one season ---------
//
// data/plants.csv carries nutrient_n / nutrient_p / nutrient_k, and they are
// NOT kilograms of anything. Until 2026-09-26 its header called them
// "kg/hectare equivalent, scaled to single plant", but tomato's 0.15 / 0.05 /
// 0.20 is no real per-plant or per-hectare figure; schemas/plant.toml and the
// Library describe them as RELATIVE demand (higher means hungrier), and that
// is how they are used here (the header now says so).
//
// So one crop with a published removal figure fixes the scale, and every
// other crop is read against it. A crop's season need, per nutrient:
//
//     need (g) = expected harvest (kg) x anchor removal (g per kg)
//                x (crop's plants.csv index / anchor's plants.csv index)
//
// The expected harvest is the middle of the crop's plants.csv yield range times
// the items.csv mass of what it harvests into, so a crop that yields more
// takes more out, and the index sets how rich that harvest is in each
// nutrient relative to a tomato. For the tomato itself: yield 2 to 8 fruit of
// 0.2 kg = 1.0 kg, so its season need is exactly the removal below.
//
// Removal, not whole-plant uptake: the soil loses what LEAVES in the harvest.
// Stems, leaves and roots are assumed to stay in the unit or go back through
// the composter (CDFA below: the fruit is about two thirds of the crop's
// above-ground N). This is the basis the Wisconsin guide uses for maintaining
// P and K.
//
// Tomato, fresh fruit. 1 lb per short ton (2,000 lb) = 0.5 g per kg.
//   N:    3 lb N/ton = 1.5 g/kg. California Department of Food and Agriculture,
//         FREP California Fertilization Guidelines, "Processing Tomatoes"
//         (https://www.cdfa.ca.gov/is/ffldrs/frep/FertilizationGuidelines/Tomato.html,
//         read 2026-09-26): "the fruits contained on average 3 lbs N/ton, which
//         accounted for about two thirds of the total N in the aboveground biomass".
//   P2O5: 1.8 lb/ton = 0.9 g/kg. University of Wisconsin-Madison Extension,
//         "Nutrient application guidelines for field, vegetable, and fruit crops
//         in Wisconsin" (A2809), Table 4.2, row "Tomato": crop removal 1.8 lb P2O5
//         and 8 lb K2O per ton fresh
//         (https://cropsandsoils.extension.wisc.edu/files/2024/07/Table-4.2.pdf,
//         read 2026-09-26).
//   K2O:  8 lb/ton = 4.0 g/kg. Same table and row.
//   Range, for the record: Oregon State EM 9165 (Sullivan et al. 2017) Table 3
//   has a 12 ton/acre tomato removing 10 lb P2O5 and 80 lb K2O, which is 0.83
//   and 6.7 lb/ton; the CDFA page gives 4 to 6 lb K/ton (4.8 to 7.2 lb K2O).
//   The Wisconsin figures sit at the top of those ranges and are one table.
//
// WHAT THIS GETS WRONG, stated so nobody trusts it further than it goes: the
// plants.csv indices do not track real removal between crop kinds. Read this
// way, a wheat unit (7 kg of grain) needs 7.0 g N, 5.0 g P2O5 and 8.4 g K2O a
// season, where Wisconsin A2809 puts wheat grain at 0.5 lb P2O5 and 0.35 lb
// K2O per 60 lb bushel (58 g and 41 g for 7 kg) and grain protein puts its N
// near 20 g/kg (about 140 g). Grain and dry legumes are dense in nutrients and
// the indices were not built from that. The real fix is to replace the three
// plants.csv columns with per-crop removal in g per kg of harvest, from tables
// like A2809's, and drop the index ratio; the formula above then loses one term.
//
// -- fertilizers: what one item puts into a unit -----------------------------
//
// Each entry names an items.csv item and its analysis as a PERCENT OF THE
// ITEM'S AS-IS (WET) MASS, plus the share of each nutrient that is plant
// available in the season it goes on. The item's mass comes from items.csv,
// so the grams follow the bag if its size ever changes. The automatic feeder
// (the "nutrient" slider on towers and fish tanks) uses EVERY entry, in the
// order listed: each tops the unit up for the nutrients in its `dose_for`
// (all three when absent), and the next covers what is still short. So urine
// goes first, dosed for nitrogen alone, and compost after it covers the
// phosphorus and potassium urine is thin in. The Fertilize button uses
// compost first, as it always has, and the next listed fertilizer the player
// carries when they have no compost. Add a fertilizer here, with its source,
// and fertilizing and the feeder pick it up.
//
// Optional fields, with their defaults:
//   organic_rest: false  - true when the N that is NOT available in the first
//                          season is organic N that releases over later years
//                          (see organic_n_release below). False means it is
//                          simply not counted.
//   dose_for: []         - the nutrients ("n", "p2o5", "k2o") the feeder
//                          doses this fertilizer by. Empty means all three:
//                          it is dosed for whichever it lacks most.
//   withhold_days: 0.0   - garden days before a crop is ripe after which this
//                          fertilizer may no longer go on it.
//
// fertilizer_0 is the composter's finished compost (data/recipes.csv: sawdust
// with seed press cake or bran, olive or apple pomace), a 2.0 kg bag. Source for
// all six numbers: Washington State University Snohomish County Extension,
// "Recommended Best Management Practices for Compost Use (Working Draft)",
// January 2016, WSU Compost Outreach Project, Table 1, worked example for a
// "Food & Yard Waste Compost": 18.8 lb N, 6.4 lb P2O5 and 11.6 lb K2O per wet
// ton as-is (0.94%, 0.32%, 0.58%), "Plant availability of N in compost: 7
// Percent", and on page 4: "Compost will provide approximately 1.3 lb Total N
// /wet ton compost, 6.4 lb P2O5/wet ton of compost, and 11.6 lb K2O/wet ton of
// compost in the first season after application". It assumes 50% moisture, so
// on a dry basis that compost is 1.88% N, 0.64% P2O5, 1.16% K2O: inside the 1
// to 3% N of Oregon State EM 9165 and near the "about 1-1-1 (N-P2O5-K2O)" of
// UMass Extension's "Compost Use and Soil Fertility" (Mangan, Barker, Bodine
// and Borten, updated 2013).
//
// One 2.0 kg bag therefore adds 18.8 g of N, of which 1.3 g is available this
// season, plus 6.4 g P2O5 and 11.6 g K2O. N availability is a range: EM 9165
// Table 10 gives 5 to 10% at one year, UMass "10% to 30%" in one season; 7% is
// the WSU worksheet's value, inside both. The other 93% of the N is organic
// and releases over the years after (organic_rest: true, schedule below), so
// compost is a weak nitrogen source in its first season and a slowly growing
// one when it is applied year after year.
//
// urine_stored_0 is one PERSON-DAY of urine from the household's sealed
// collection tank (1.5 kg, items.csv). A person-day, not a litre, because the
// source says to count it that way: "the calculation should preferably be
// based upon the number of persons and days that it has been collected from,
// as this gives a better indication of the nutrient content than the volume"
// (Jonsson et al. 2004, below, p. 7). Where the household's urine comes from
// in the game: src/systems/food.rs, the Compost action.
//
// Source for N and P: Jonsson, H., Richert Stintzing, A., Vinneras, B. and
// Salomon, E. (2004), "Guidelines on the Use of Urine and Faeces in Crop
// Production", EcoSanRes Publications Series, Report 2004-2, Stockholm
// Environment Institute, Table 1, "Proposed new Swedish default values for
// excreted mass and nutrients (Vinneras, 2002)": urine 550 kg wet mass, 4000 g
// N and 365 g P per person per year. The same table is reproduced as Table 2
// in Richert et al. (2010), "Practical Guidance on the Use of Urine in Crop
// Production", EcoSanRes Series 2010-1. So N is 4000 / 550000 = 0.727% of the
// urine's mass (7.3 g per litre, at the top of the guide's "3-7 g N per
// litre" rule of thumb), and P is 365 / 550000 x 2.29 = 0.152% as P2O5 (2.29
// converts P to P2O5, Richert et al. 2010 Table 6). A person-day carries 10.9 g
// N, 2.3 g P2O5 and 3.6 g K2O.
//
// Source for K: the Swedish table gives none, so Jonsson et al. 2004 Table 3
// ("Estimated excretion of nutrients per capita in different countries"):
// urine K of 1.3 (China), 0.9 (Haiti), 1.1 (India), 1.2 (South Africa) and 1.0
// (Uganda) kg per person per year. The median, 1.1 kg, is 1100 / 550000 x 1.2
// = 0.24% as K2O (1.2 converts K to K2O, Richert et al. 2010 Table 6). Mixing a
// Swedish N and P with a five-country K is a stated choice: the K range is
// narrow and Sweden's own K is not in the source.
//
// Availability, all 1.0, in the source's words (p. 9): "The plant
// availability of urine N is the same as that of chemical urea or ammonium
// fertilizers"; urine P availability "has been found to be at least as good
// as that of chemical phosphate"; and "K is excreted in the urine as ions,
// which are directly plant-available". The N stays in the urine only if the
// tank is sealed: stored urine loses ammonia when ventilated, and "these losses
// are easily eliminated by designing the system so that the tank and pipes are
// not ventilated, but just pressure equalized" (p. 11). The game assumes a
// sealed tank and urine worked into the soil when applied (p. 1: "urine should
// be applied close to the soil and incorporated as soon as possible"); it does
// not model the loss when either rule is broken.
//
// dose_for n: urine is dosed at a nitrogen fertilizer's rate (p. 1:
// "Application rates for commercial mineral nitrogen fertilizers (urea or
// ammonium), if available, can be used as a basis for recommendations on the
// use of urine").
//
// withhold_days 30, the SAFETY RULE. The WHO guidelines as reproduced in
// Richert et al. 2010 (p. 27, "Withholding period"): "The time between urine
// application and harvest should be at least one month." Table 16 there
// (WHO 2006, Guidelines for the Safe Use of Wastewater, Excreta and
// Greywater, Volume 4) sets storage of 1 to 6 months, by temperature and crop,
// for LARGER systems, meaning "a system where the urine mixture is used to
// fertilize crops that will be consumed by individuals other than members of
// the household from which the urine was collected"; for a household's own
// urine on its own food the recommended storage is "0-1 month" (p. 25). The
// family home feeds its own household, so the game applies the one-month
// withholding period and no storage wait: urine is used straight from the
// sealed tank, but never on a crop within 30 garden days of ripening. Selling
// urine-fed crops to other people would, by WHO's own line, call for the
// longer stored-urine rules, which the game does not model.
(
    demand_anchor: (
        plant: "tomato",
        n_g_per_kg: 1.5,
        p2o5_g_per_kg: 0.9,
        k2o_g_per_kg: 4.0,
    ),
    fertilizers: [
        (
            item: "urine_stored_0",
            n_pct: 0.727,
            p2o5_pct: 0.152,
            k2o_pct: 0.24,
            n_available: 1.0,
            p2o5_available: 1.0,
            k2o_available: 1.0,
            dose_for: ["n"],
            withhold_days: 30.0,
        ),
        (
            item: "fertilizer_0",
            n_pct: 0.94,
            p2o5_pct: 0.32,
            k2o_pct: 0.58,
            n_available: 0.07,
            p2o5_available: 1.0,
            k2o_available: 1.0,
            organic_rest: true,
        ),
    ],
    // -- organic_n_release: how compost's organic N comes back -------------
    //
    // The share of the organic N STILL IN THE SOIL that is released in each
    // year after it goes on, as percent: the first number is its first year,
    // the second its second year, and the last number holds for every year
    // after that. Released N becomes plant-available in the unit.
    //
    // The first year is 0 here only because compost's first-season release
    // (7% of its N, WSU above) is already credited the moment it is applied.
    //
    // Source: Gravuer, K. (2016), "Compost Application Rates for California
    // Croplands and Rangelands for a CDFA Healthy Soils Incentives Program",
    // University of California, Davis, for the California Department of Food
    // and Agriculture, Draft Version 2.0, 22 July 2016, p. 8, from its
    // literature review: "Each subsequent year, additional remaining
    // organically-bound nitrogen would be mineralized, at a rate that would
    // decline by half each year to a minimum of approximately 2% until all of
    // the organic N in the compost had been consumed". Its worked example:
    // 10% in the first year, "5% of the remaining organically-bound nitrogen
    // in the second year, 2.5% of the remaining organically-bound nitrogen in
    // the third year, and 2% of the remaining organically-bound nitrogen in
    // the fourth year and subsequent years" (for lower-N compost, "2-7%
    // (average 5%)" in the first year, "with a similar pattern").
    //
    // Applied to this compost's 7% first year, the halving rule gives 3.5% in
    // the second year, and 1.75% in the third, which the 2% floor lifts to 2%,
    // held from then on. Cross-check on the floor: Oregon State EM 9165
    // (Sullivan, Peachey, Heinrich and Brewer, May 2017), p. 18: "Only 2 to 3
    // percent of total soil organic matter is mineralized each year", which is
    // what old compost has become.
    //
    // A "year" is 365 GARDEN days: the unit's crop growth clock, the same clock
    // the crops draw on (it pauses in the dark and runs ten times as fast at
    // the 10x growth setting). The pool releases only while a crop grows in
    // the unit. Nothing is lost from it: the game does not model leaching or
    // denitrification, which in a real soil take part of this N.
    organic_n_release: (
        yearly_pct: [0.0, 3.5, 2.0],
    ),
    // -- legume_harvest_n_share: what a legume's harvest carries away -------
    //
    // A legume gets part of its N from the air (plants.csv n_fixed_pct, the
    // %Ndfa), so it draws only the rest from its unit. When it is harvested,
    // its roots, nodules and haulm stay behind holding N, and the FIXED share
    // of that N is new to the unit: a credit for the next crop. This number is
    // the share of a grain legume's whole-plant N, roots included, that leaves
    // with the harvest; the credit is then
    //
    //     fixed share x removal x (1 / 0.55 - 1)
    //
    // where removal is the crop's season need before fixation.
    //
    // Source: Salvagiotti, F., Cassman, K.G., Specht, J.E., Walters, D.T.,
    // Weiss, A. and Dobermann, A. (2008), "Nitrogen uptake, fixation and
    // response to fertilizer N in soybeans: A review", Field Crops Research
    // 108: 1-13, abstract (637 data sets): "On an average, 50-60% of soybean N
    // demand was met by biological N2 fixation" and "The partial N balance
    // (fixed N in above-ground biomass - N in seeds) was negative in 80% of all
    // data sets ... However, when an average estimated below-ground N
    // contribution of 24% of total plant N was included, the average N balance
    // was close to neutral (-4 kg N ha-1)". A neutral balance means the fixed
    // N equals the N the seed carried away, so the seed carried the same share
    // of the whole plant's N as fixation supplied: 50 to 60%, taken as 0.55.
    // With soybean's own fixed share (55%) the credit then equals what it drew
    // from the unit, and the unit ends the season where it started, which is
    // the review's finding. A stronger fixer (faba bean) leaves the unit
    // richer, a weak one (common bean) poorer, as New Mexico State's Guide
    // A-129 (Flynn and Idowu, rev. June 2015) says of them: common beans "fix
    // less than their nitrogen needs", and "When the grain from a grain legume
    // crop is harvested, little nitrogen is returned for the following crop."
    //
    // Soybean's share is used for every legume because no per-crop figure was
    // found with roots included; a legume picked green (snap beans, fresh
    // peas) leaves more of its N behind than this says. The credit goes into
    // the unit's store as available N for the next crop sown there (NMSU A-129:
    // "Most of the nitrogen eventually returns to the soil for neighboring
    // plants when vegetation (roots, leaves, fruits) of the legume dies and
    // decomposes").
    legume_harvest_n_share: 0.55,
)
